WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.577 --> 00:00:02.918
- He's an American actor, director,

00:00:02.918 --> 00:00:04.956
screenwriter and producer.

00:00:04.956 --> 00:00:07.374
He's best known for
portraying Walter White

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:10.657
in the AMC crime drama
series Breaking Bad.

00:00:10.657 --> 00:00:12.945
He won four Primetime Emmy Awards for

00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:15.715
Outstanding Lead Actor in a Drama Series.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:17.771
He's Bryan Cranston and here are his

00:00:17.771 --> 00:00:20.129
top ten rules for success.

00:00:20.129 --> 00:00:21.227
(airplane flying)

00:00:21.227 --> 00:00:22.486
(basketball bounce)

00:00:22.486 --> 00:00:26.971
- I started working in
this business in 1979

00:00:26.971 --> 00:00:29.399
so I've been doing it for 32 years.

00:00:29.399 --> 00:00:32.894
- And you got steady work
you had lots of gigs.

00:00:32.894 --> 00:00:34.812
- [Bryan] Yeah but but the last ten years

00:00:34.812 --> 00:00:36.001
have been just crazy.

00:00:36.001 --> 00:00:37.567
You know I think I think

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:40.629
there is truth in the
saying that you have to

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:42.558
know when it's your time.

00:00:42.558 --> 00:00:44.456
And sometimes you know people go through

00:00:44.456 --> 00:00:46.646
their awkward moments in high school

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:49.908
and they feel gangly
and they're not you know

00:00:49.908 --> 00:00:52.644
they're not sure of themselves and things.

00:00:52.644 --> 00:00:53.768
This seems to be my time.

00:00:53.768 --> 00:00:57.101
It waited awhile but I'm glad it's here.

00:00:58.072 --> 00:01:01.989
Well the best the best
advice for fellow actors

00:01:03.676 --> 00:01:06.308
is this, know what your job is.

00:01:06.308 --> 00:01:09.647
About 18 years ago I had
this cognition that I

00:01:09.647 --> 00:01:12.413
realized I was going into auditions

00:01:12.413 --> 00:01:13.996
trying to get a job

00:01:15.629 --> 00:01:18.288
and that simply wasn't what I was doing.

00:01:18.288 --> 00:01:21.014
It wasn't what I said what
I'm supposed to be doing.

00:01:21.014 --> 00:01:23.760
An actor is supposed
to create a compelling

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.440
and interesting character

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.868
that serves the text we presented in the

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:30.451
environment where you audition.

00:01:31.842 --> 00:01:33.615
And then you walk away

00:01:33.615 --> 00:01:34.489
and that's it.

00:01:34.489 --> 00:01:37.067
Everything else is out of your control

00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:39.422
so don't even think of,
don't focus on that.

00:01:39.422 --> 00:01:41.540
You're not going there to get a job

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.881
you're going there to present what you do.

00:01:44.881 --> 00:01:48.240
You act and there it is and walk away.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.696
There's power in that,
there's confidence in that.

00:01:51.696 --> 00:01:54.898
And it's also saying I can only do so much

00:01:54.898 --> 00:01:58.027
and then the decision
of who might get a job

00:01:58.027 --> 00:02:00.960
is so out of your control
that it can really

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.986
when you analyze it it makes
no sense to hold on to that.

00:02:04.986 --> 00:02:08.122
That to me was a breakthrough.

00:02:08.122 --> 00:02:12.678
And once I adapted that
philosophy I never looked back

00:02:12.678 --> 00:02:15.268
and I've never been busier in my life.

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:17.248
And once I grabbed onto that.

00:02:17.248 --> 00:02:21.318
For me when I first read the
first page of the pilot script

00:02:21.318 --> 00:02:23.753
it read just like you saw,

00:02:23.753 --> 00:02:27.738
opened up on a beautiful
setting, red mountains,

00:02:27.738 --> 00:02:30.442
trousers are falling from the sky.

00:02:30.442 --> 00:02:33.308
They hit the ground,
an RV rolls over them.

00:02:33.308 --> 00:02:35.847
Inside there's a
middle-aged man only wearing

00:02:35.847 --> 00:02:37.500
tighty whitey underwears

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.691
and a respirator driving madly in the back

00:02:39.691 --> 00:02:42.417
two dead bodies sliding
up and back in a sea

00:02:42.417 --> 00:02:43.870
of fluid and glass.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:46.228
Another man passed out with a respirator.

00:02:46.228 --> 00:02:48.949
And I'm thinking what
the fuck is going on?

00:02:48.949 --> 00:02:51.299
(laughing)

00:02:51.299 --> 00:02:54.678
That was page one.
(laughing)

00:02:54.678 --> 00:02:57.575
And it took off from there and so it

00:02:57.575 --> 00:03:00.852
was it was one of those those rare moments

00:03:00.852 --> 00:03:02.435
when you respond to

00:03:03.358 --> 00:03:05.874
a piece of material so strongly

00:03:05.874 --> 00:03:09.529
that I knew I had to get
in as fast as I could

00:03:09.529 --> 00:03:11.429
to try to get this role.

00:03:11.429 --> 00:03:15.443
Because the the longer I
waited I knew that every actor

00:03:15.443 --> 00:03:18.646
in Hollywood would want to
would want to be a part of this.

00:03:18.646 --> 00:03:20.191
And fortunately I was the one to get it.

00:03:20.191 --> 00:03:23.441
Being able to display all that you feel

00:03:24.394 --> 00:03:27.029
is not unlike a person wanting to

00:03:27.029 --> 00:03:29.244
experience everything in life.

00:03:29.244 --> 00:03:31.627
You don't always want
to be serious you don't

00:03:31.627 --> 00:03:33.149
always want to be silly.

00:03:33.149 --> 00:03:36.594
You want to have moments
in each, for me anyway.

00:03:36.594 --> 00:03:38.865
And so that's why I look
for different projects

00:03:38.865 --> 00:03:43.032
different things that take
me to more challenging areas

00:03:44.606 --> 00:03:47.900
difficult or easy or fun, simple.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:51.817
Sometimes incredibly
complex like Breaking Bad.

00:03:52.746 --> 00:03:57.335
I had a cognitive moment
when I was 22 years old

00:03:57.335 --> 00:03:59.870
and my brother and I
were riding motorcycles

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:01.657
across the country for a couple years

00:04:01.657 --> 00:04:03.388
because we were both trying to figure out

00:04:03.388 --> 00:04:05.962
what we wanted to do with our lives.

00:04:05.962 --> 00:04:08.102
And it was raining, I was
on the Blue Ridge Parkway

00:04:08.102 --> 00:04:11.102
in Virginia and it was raining hard.

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:15.734
So we found this little
off the beaten path

00:04:16.716 --> 00:04:19.383
rest stop that was near a creek.

00:04:20.316 --> 00:04:24.990
And there was a slab of
cement with four wooden posts

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.490
and a roof and a picnic table.

00:04:28.368 --> 00:04:31.206
At this rest stop no one else was there

00:04:31.206 --> 00:04:32.039
it was pouring rain.

00:04:32.039 --> 00:04:34.877
So we pulled our motorcycles
and jumped up the curb

00:04:34.877 --> 00:04:36.980
right up onto it.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.223
It looked like we were going
to be there for a while

00:04:39.223 --> 00:04:42.937
so we set up a camp on
this slab of cement.

00:04:42.937 --> 00:04:44.955
And it stayed there for six days

00:04:44.955 --> 00:04:47.079
because it didn't stop raining.

00:04:47.079 --> 00:04:49.625
And over the six days the
only thing that really got me

00:04:49.625 --> 00:04:53.034
through was reading a book
of a thick book of plays

00:04:53.034 --> 00:04:55.341
it was on a motorcycle
so I couldn't take a lot.

00:04:55.341 --> 00:04:58.377
So I had this book of plays
and I was reading Hedda Gabler

00:04:58.377 --> 00:05:01.118
Ibsen's Hedda Gabler at the time.

00:05:01.118 --> 00:05:04.957
And and all of a sudden it
was nightfall, it was dark.

00:05:04.957 --> 00:05:08.814
I went what and I realized
wow that went fast

00:05:08.814 --> 00:05:12.694
because I was so into
the reading and I thought

00:05:12.694 --> 00:05:14.011
man that would be.

00:05:14.011 --> 00:05:17.636
And it just went everything
lifted and I went

00:05:17.636 --> 00:05:18.931
that's what I should do.

00:05:18.931 --> 00:05:21.348
I loved getting lost in story

00:05:22.761 --> 00:05:25.626
so I want to tell stories.

00:05:25.626 --> 00:05:28.405
And I had done some acting and I thought

00:05:28.405 --> 00:05:30.554
that's what I need to do I need to

00:05:30.554 --> 00:05:32.971
follow that lead get involved

00:05:33.849 --> 00:05:35.657
in something that I love to do

00:05:35.657 --> 00:05:37.427
and hopefully become good at it.

00:05:37.427 --> 00:05:39.467
As opposed to getting
trapped into something

00:05:39.467 --> 00:05:41.824
I happen to be good at but I didn't love.

00:05:41.824 --> 00:05:44.964
The success of an actor
is being willing to

00:05:44.964 --> 00:05:47.637
go into those dark places and and

00:05:47.637 --> 00:05:49.470
display them publicly.

00:05:51.959 --> 00:05:55.991
We have to have that kind
of courage and vulnerability

00:05:55.991 --> 00:05:56.929
to do that.

00:05:56.929 --> 00:06:00.023
In high school all the way
through school I remember

00:06:00.023 --> 00:06:02.866
trying to stay the same as everyone else.

00:06:02.866 --> 00:06:05.864
I didn't want to stand out
everyone were wearing that

00:06:05.864 --> 00:06:07.706
and this is what everybody else is wearing

00:06:07.706 --> 00:06:11.922
I kind of want to I don't want
to be isolated or ostracized.

00:06:11.922 --> 00:06:14.079
Certainly I don't want to be
embarrassed or have someone

00:06:14.079 --> 00:06:18.164
point the finger at you,
look at that guy, you know.

00:06:18.164 --> 00:06:21.405
What happens is that we think
that if you are vulnerable

00:06:21.405 --> 00:06:26.004
and sensitive and open
that you'll be ridiculed.

00:06:26.004 --> 00:06:29.691
And but as adults when we
mature what we realize is

00:06:29.691 --> 00:06:33.987
that if you have the audacity
to display those human

00:06:33.987 --> 00:06:38.046
honest emotions that the
opposite is what happens.

00:06:38.046 --> 00:06:41.463
People embrace someone who is in trouble,

00:06:42.673 --> 00:06:45.602
someone who is vulnerable,
someone who is frightened.

00:06:45.602 --> 00:06:49.005
We they they have a tendency
to come forward and put

00:06:49.005 --> 00:06:51.771
their emotional arms around you.

00:06:51.771 --> 00:06:56.770
As an actor you need to to study
human behavior in some ways

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:59.187
in order to do that you test.

00:07:00.572 --> 00:07:02.460
You behave a certain way

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.397
and the response to that informs you

00:07:05.397 --> 00:07:07.541
whether it's working or not.

00:07:07.541 --> 00:07:10.918
You know I think in ways
without me consciously

00:07:10.918 --> 00:07:14.919
knowing it I'm always kind of
experimenting with characters

00:07:14.919 --> 00:07:19.086
in waves and seeing how it
feels to be a certain way.

00:07:21.616 --> 00:07:22.862
It's intriguing.

00:07:22.862 --> 00:07:25.291
Shirley Knight was was
interesting because she got me

00:07:25.291 --> 00:07:29.839
over a hump where I was
putting up resistance

00:07:29.839 --> 00:07:32.258
to be you know to say you know I was

00:07:32.258 --> 00:07:34.104
getting some work and I was starting

00:07:34.104 --> 00:07:37.251
I was working exclusively
as an actor since I was

00:07:37.251 --> 00:07:39.095
25 years old, 24 years old.

00:07:39.095 --> 00:07:42.349
I've never done anything
else from that point on.

00:07:42.349 --> 00:07:44.266
And I came to her later

00:07:45.848 --> 00:07:49.233
and I was working as an
actor and she said, "Own it."

00:07:49.233 --> 00:07:52.656
You know because I was putting
it, oh no, I don't want to be

00:07:52.656 --> 00:07:55.658
I don't want to be a star
I don't want to because.

00:07:55.658 --> 00:07:58.175
Let all that go and just again

00:07:58.175 --> 00:08:00.917
philosophy let it come in let it come on.

00:08:00.917 --> 00:08:03.343
Whatever's supposed to
happen happens and let it go.

00:08:03.343 --> 00:08:06.082
You don't have to hold on
to it and hold a mirror

00:08:06.082 --> 00:08:07.864
up to yourself but

00:08:07.864 --> 00:08:10.744
but just let it let it flow.

00:08:10.744 --> 00:08:14.613
And she she had a point
that was very interesting,

00:08:14.613 --> 00:08:18.446
to to accept and embrace
an actor's arrogance.

00:08:20.603 --> 00:08:22.839
And at at first you, arrogance oh,

00:08:22.839 --> 00:08:24.438
I don't want to be arrogant.

00:08:24.438 --> 00:08:25.932
And she goes, "No, but it's okay.

00:08:25.932 --> 00:08:26.765
"Because actors like athletes
are the ones who say,

00:08:26.765 --> 00:08:29.848
"give me the ball three seconds left.

00:08:31.847 --> 00:08:33.801
"I want to take the shot.

00:08:33.801 --> 00:08:36.785
"Or whoever is best to take
the shot you take the shot.

00:08:36.785 --> 00:08:39.276
"Well we'll do all we can
to make sure that you get."

00:08:39.276 --> 00:08:42.197
You know whatever the case
but you want that shot.

00:08:42.197 --> 00:08:44.456
And then there are some kids you know who,

00:08:44.456 --> 00:08:46.409
"Don't give me the ball,
don't send it to me,

00:08:46.409 --> 00:08:48.671
"don't leave it to me I don't
want that responsibility.

00:08:48.671 --> 00:08:49.694
"I don't want that pressure."

00:08:49.694 --> 00:08:52.021
And an actor has to be the
type of person that says,

00:08:52.021 --> 00:08:53.638
"Give it to me, let me try this."

00:08:53.638 --> 00:08:54.471
Takes a chance.

00:08:54.471 --> 00:08:56.851
You have to have a healthy dose of luck

00:08:56.851 --> 00:08:58.048
to become successful.

00:08:58.048 --> 00:09:00.284
That's just the way it is.

00:09:00.284 --> 00:09:02.942
You can't you can't prepare for it

00:09:02.942 --> 00:09:05.888
but you can be ready for
it if it does come to you.

00:09:05.888 --> 00:09:09.920
I was very lucky to get
that role on X-Files

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:13.685
simply because I was
physically in the city

00:09:13.685 --> 00:09:15.789
where it was casting.

00:09:15.789 --> 00:09:19.003
And I was out of the city
for three weeks prior

00:09:19.003 --> 00:09:22.339
and I very could have
easily not been in town

00:09:22.339 --> 00:09:24.899
and they just go on and someone
else would have got the role

00:09:24.899 --> 00:09:26.541
and who knows where I would have been.

00:09:26.541 --> 00:09:30.441
I certainly odds are very high
that I wouldn't have gotten

00:09:30.441 --> 00:09:34.590
Breaking Bad had I not
met Vince Gilligan before.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:37.049
So it's one of those things where you you

00:09:37.049 --> 00:09:39.774
have to just do your
wor, do the best you can

00:09:39.774 --> 00:09:42.748
and hope for the lucky
breaks here and there.

00:09:42.748 --> 00:09:45.069
- In magic the most
important thing to a magician

00:09:45.069 --> 00:09:46.668
is his hands.

00:09:46.668 --> 00:09:49.533
You know everything
that you do is based on

00:09:49.533 --> 00:09:52.845
being able to manipulate and right.

00:09:52.845 --> 00:09:55.769
So this type of stuff it looks
simple but it's actually me

00:09:55.769 --> 00:09:57.547
working for a long time to figure out

00:09:57.547 --> 00:09:59.508
how to do this stuff and it's practice

00:09:59.508 --> 00:10:02.075
it's hours and hours
and hours of practice.

00:10:02.075 --> 00:10:05.257
So the one thing that a magician
I think values more than

00:10:05.257 --> 00:10:08.593
anything else is just the hands.

00:10:08.593 --> 00:10:12.112
You're going to be the one
picking the cup so that

00:10:12.112 --> 00:10:15.779
the fate of my hands
lies in your decisions.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:30.147
Can you point to one of the four cups?

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:33.651
- How much is riding on this?

00:10:33.651 --> 00:10:36.641
- Everything.
- Everything.

00:10:36.641 --> 00:10:38.058
- That one.
- Yes.

00:10:40.096 --> 00:10:42.846
(dramatic music)

00:10:44.973 --> 00:10:47.473
- Oh.
- That was a good guess.

00:10:49.639 --> 00:10:52.389
(dramatic music)

00:10:54.731 --> 00:10:58.857
I saw your reaction.
(laughing)

00:10:58.857 --> 00:11:00.107
- [Woman] Ohhh.

00:11:01.753 --> 00:11:03.253
- But the truth is

00:11:05.933 --> 00:11:10.157
maybe it doesn't matter
one way or another.

00:11:10.157 --> 00:11:13.074
Like ultimately when we watch magic

00:11:15.233 --> 00:11:17.483
the problem with this trick

00:11:18.631 --> 00:11:20.714
is you don't want this to

00:11:22.243 --> 00:11:26.652
really succeed because
no personal curiosity is

00:11:26.652 --> 00:11:30.912
what would happen if that
went through the hand.

00:11:30.912 --> 00:11:31.745
- Ohhhhhh

00:11:34.634 --> 00:11:36.892
- See how that really looks

00:11:36.892 --> 00:11:39.186
like it's going through the hand?

00:11:39.186 --> 00:11:40.628
- Yeah

00:11:40.628 --> 00:11:42.564
- And does it really
look like it's actually.

00:11:42.564 --> 00:11:44.958
- It is yeah, oh God no.

00:11:44.958 --> 00:11:46.460
- Oh wow.

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:48.377
- But there's no blood.

00:11:49.217 --> 00:11:51.550
(screaming)

00:11:57.298 --> 00:11:58.773
- No way!

00:11:58.773 --> 00:12:00.106
Are you, oh God.

00:12:00.941 --> 00:12:03.034
That is through your hand.

00:12:03.034 --> 00:12:05.007
- There's no blood.

00:12:05.007 --> 00:12:06.229
- Why?

00:12:06.229 --> 00:12:08.062
It it, just pull that.

00:12:10.684 --> 00:12:11.601
- Oh oh oh.

00:12:13.403 --> 00:12:14.461
- Pull.

00:12:14.461 --> 00:12:16.391
- It went through your hand.

00:12:16.391 --> 00:12:19.552
It went through your
hand, this is not a glove.

00:12:19.552 --> 00:12:20.385
(banging)

00:12:20.385 --> 00:12:21.968
It doesn't retract.

00:12:23.008 --> 00:12:23.841
Oh my God.

00:12:24.697 --> 00:12:26.797
- Wow wow that's the
craziest thing I've ever

00:12:26.797 --> 00:12:28.964
seen in my life obviously.

00:12:30.693 --> 00:12:31.526
- I can't.

00:12:32.714 --> 00:12:35.547
- You're out of your mind and most

00:12:36.483 --> 00:12:39.016
you're just out of your minds.

00:12:39.016 --> 00:12:41.385
In the most beautiful way possible.

00:12:41.385 --> 00:12:42.218
My God.

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:45.142
- Thank you so much for
watching I made this video

00:12:45.142 --> 00:12:47.898
because Orion Dark Knight asked me to.

00:12:47.898 --> 00:12:49.526
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you

00:12:49.526 --> 00:12:51.531
want me to profile next leave it in the

00:12:51.531 --> 00:12:53.301
comments below and I'll see what I can do.

00:12:53.301 --> 00:12:57.038
I'd also love to know
what your Bryan Cranston's

00:12:57.038 --> 00:13:00.366
top 10 rules meant the most to you.

00:13:00.366 --> 00:13:02.338
Leave it in the comments and
I'll join in the discussion.

00:13:02.338 --> 00:13:03.769
Thank you so much for watching
and continue to believe

00:13:03.769 --> 00:13:05.351
and I'll see you soon

00:13:05.351 --> 00:13:06.184
(airplane flying)

00:13:06.184 --> 00:13:07.712
(basketball bounce)

00:13:07.712 --> 00:13:10.243
(whoosh)

00:13:10.243 --> 00:13:12.136
- And when you're playing a guy who's

00:13:12.136 --> 00:13:14.703
afflicted with cancer you know
the people watching the show

00:13:14.703 --> 00:13:16.095
a lot of your audience has cancer.

00:13:16.095 --> 00:13:17.337
- Yeah.
- Like that's just

00:13:17.337 --> 00:13:19.503
the nature of this new
world we live in right now.

00:13:19.503 --> 00:13:21.465
How do you play a guy like
that when you know that

00:13:21.465 --> 00:13:23.764
there are people who
are really feeling it?

00:13:23.764 --> 00:13:26.276
- Well the the responsibility of an actor

00:13:26.276 --> 00:13:28.864
is to is to be truthful

00:13:28.864 --> 00:13:30.796
in how you portray that.

00:13:30.796 --> 00:13:33.834
So one of the one of the
simple things is I knew

00:13:33.834 --> 00:13:36.595
that when my character
was going to go through

00:13:36.595 --> 00:13:39.951
chemotherapy and radiation is
that he would lose his hair.

00:13:39.951 --> 00:13:41.532
So I shaved my head.

00:13:41.532 --> 00:13:45.567
I also knew that from personal
experience in my family

00:13:45.567 --> 00:13:49.896
that the chemotherapy
and primarily makes you

00:13:49.896 --> 00:13:53.342
lose your appetite and it
all tastes like metal inside

00:13:53.342 --> 00:13:55.977
when you're going through that treatment.

00:13:55.977 --> 00:13:57.877
And so you lose weight.

00:13:57.877 --> 00:14:00.294
I lost 16 pounds in ten days.

00:14:01.686 --> 00:14:04.214
- Hard to do that healthy.

00:14:04.214 --> 00:14:07.293
- Yeah but I did it carefully.

00:14:07.293 --> 00:14:09.868
I cut off my right leg.

00:14:09.868 --> 00:14:11.979
(laughing)
And this is a

00:14:11.979 --> 00:14:13.010
prosthetic right there.

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:14.845
- Certainly got down there yeah.

00:14:14.845 --> 00:14:16.636
- Sixteen pounds, easy.

00:14:16.636 --> 00:14:18.690
- Just put in a bag, put in the freezer

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:20.607
we can attach it later.

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:24.448
- So no I measured the time between

00:14:24.448 --> 00:14:26.603
when I would have my shirt off

00:14:26.603 --> 00:14:29.210
you know when a shower scene or something

00:14:29.210 --> 00:14:30.881
before the weight loss
and then when I was going

00:14:30.881 --> 00:14:35.230
to have my shirt off again
after the chemotherapy.

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:38.255
And it was it was 16 days oh

00:14:38.255 --> 00:14:39.921
it's in ten days

00:14:39.921 --> 00:14:42.563
so that I had to quickly drop that weight.

00:14:42.563 --> 00:14:45.978
I can tell you the the the best lesson

00:14:45.978 --> 00:14:48.894
that I've ever learned as an actor

00:14:48.894 --> 00:14:50.227
for my career is

00:14:52.125 --> 00:14:54.824
always follow the well-written word.

00:14:54.824 --> 00:14:56.631
It will never fail you.

00:14:56.631 --> 00:14:59.366
Whatever production you may be in

00:14:59.366 --> 00:15:02.335
may not necessarily be ultimately

00:15:02.335 --> 00:15:04.767
quote-unquote a success.

00:15:04.767 --> 00:15:08.446
The film, role may not
be hit at the box office

00:15:08.446 --> 00:15:11.324
but fuck that, who cares about that stuff?

00:15:11.324 --> 00:15:14.821
It's not that's not your job
to worry about those things.

00:15:14.821 --> 00:15:17.238
It's to focus on on that work

00:15:18.216 --> 00:15:19.466
and it's always

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:23.434
about the writing.

00:15:23.434 --> 00:15:26.168
I have I know that as
sure as I'm sitting here

00:15:26.168 --> 00:15:29.905
when I when I decide
to do a film or a play

00:15:29.905 --> 00:15:34.046
or whatever the first thing I
look for is the overall story.

00:15:34.046 --> 00:15:35.780
How compelling is it?

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:37.476
How rich is it?

00:15:37.476 --> 00:15:39.038
How important is it?

00:15:39.038 --> 00:15:41.162
Will this make a social statement?

00:15:41.162 --> 00:15:43.804
What, where in in line does

00:15:43.804 --> 00:15:45.709
this you know rest.

00:15:45.709 --> 00:15:47.459
The next is the text.

00:15:48.747 --> 00:15:52.200
It's either the script or the play itself.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.357
And they're not the same,
make the distinction

00:15:54.357 --> 00:15:56.781
between those things because
sometimes you can have

00:15:56.781 --> 00:15:58.135
a great story

00:15:58.135 --> 00:16:01.017
it's just not fully realized by the the

00:16:01.017 --> 00:16:04.017
script it just didn't quite hit it.

00:16:04.017 --> 00:16:07.461
Or, the script was
fantastic and I didn't think

00:16:07.461 --> 00:16:09.157
the story was going to be so good

00:16:09.157 --> 00:16:11.937
and all of a sudden now it's clear to me.

00:16:11.937 --> 00:16:15.661
- My question is because
I grew up in Albuquerque

00:16:15.661 --> 00:16:19.705
what was there any cool
places anything that you liked

00:16:19.705 --> 00:16:20.642
being there?

00:16:20.642 --> 00:16:24.245
Like how was it because it's my hometown.

00:16:24.245 --> 00:16:26.485
So I want to know, how'd you like it?

00:16:26.485 --> 00:16:28.561
Or did you have fun there?

00:16:28.561 --> 00:16:31.088
- Yeah I'd go and visit
your mother once in awhile.

00:16:31.088 --> 00:16:34.421
(laughing and applause)

